[+183s] data: {"session_id": "26dc101ae0824f959c8995bcc90ee6f8", "mode": "research", "task": "What is a vector database?", "topic_type": "general", "answer": "# What Is a Vector Database?\n\nA vector database is a specialized data store optimized for storing and querying high-dimensional embedding vectors \u2014 numerical representations of text, images, or other data \u2014 to enable fast similarity search. This technology has become central to modern AI applications such as semantic search, recommendation systems, and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) for large language models (LLMs).\n\n## Core Capabilities\n\nVector databases are designed to provide:\n- High-performance vector search and low-latency retrieval\n- Hybrid search and efficient embedding storage\n- Scalable indexing to support growing AI and multimodal workloads\n- Real-time retrieval for AI and LLM applications\n\n## Why Vector Databases Matter: Key Use Cases\n\n**Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and Semantic Search**\nOrganizations across finance, retail, healthcare, media, and technology are deploying vector databases to support RAG pipelines, semantic search, and personalized user experiences.\n\n**Enterprise Semantic Search**\nVector database capabilities align with the rising need for intelligent, scalable, and cloud-ready AI infrastructure across enterprise use cases.\n\n**Natural Language Processing (NLP)**\nNLP is a leading use case for vector databases, as these applications rely heavily on efficient storage and retrieval of text embeddings and semantic vectors. The NLP segment alone is projected to reach USD 6 billion by 2034, underscoring the foundational role vector databases play for large-scale language models and enterprise AI tools.\n\n## Market Growth and Adoption\n\nVector databases represent a fast-growing, foundational AI infrastructure category:\n\n- The global vector database market is projected to grow from USD 2,652.1 million in 2025 to USD 8,945.7 million by 2030, a CAGR of 27.5%, driven by adoption of AI, LLMs, and multimodal applications.\n- Vector database as a service (cloud-based deployment) is projected to grow from $1.24 billion in 2024 to $1.62 billion in 2025, a CAGR of 30.7%, as cloud environments increasingly rely on scalable vector search for intelligent data retrieval, personalization, and AI-driven analytics.\n- North America currently dominates the market, holding a 36.6% revenue share.\n\n## Industry Adoption\n\nEstablished database and cloud vendors are actively building native vector search capabilities. For example, in May 2025, Teradata Corporation launched its Enterprise Vector Store, an in-database solution unifying structured and unstructured data while delivering sub-second query performance \u2014 illustrating how traditional database vendors are racing to compete in this space.\n\n## Competitive Landscape\n\nThe vector database market includes both dedicated startups and established cloud/database giants. Key players named across market reports include Microsoft, Elastic, MongoDB, Google, AWS, Redis, Alibaba Cloud, DataStax, SingleStore, Pinecone, Zilliz, KX, Marqo.ai, ActiveLoop, Supabase, Jina AI, Typesense, Weaviate, GSI Technology, Kinetica, Qdrant, ClickHouse, OpenSearch, Vespa.ai, and LanceDB.\n\n## Summary\n\nVector databases have emerged as a fast-growing, foundational category of AI infrastructure, enabling the storage and retrieval of embedding vectors that power semantic search, recommendation systems, and RAG-based LLM applications. Strong market growth projections, active product launches from major vendors, and a diverse competitive landscape spanning startups and established cloud providers all point to vector databases' central role in supporting the current wave of AI adoption.", "approved": true, "forced_stop_reason": "", "revision_count": 2, "max_revisions": 2, "iterations": 9, "max_iterations": 12, "retries": 0, "usage": {"input_tokens": 37912, "output_tokens": 7364, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "web_search_requests": 2, "web_fetch_requests": 0, "calls": 8, "cost_usd": 0.22345998, "embedding_tokens": 733, "embedding_requests": 2, "embedding_cost_usd": 4.398e-05, "pricing_unknown": false}, "cost_usd": 0.22346, "trace": [{"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "classifier"}, {"node": "classifier", "topic_type": "general"}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "researcher"}, {"node": "researcher", "notes_length": 3417, "recalled_from_memory": 0}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "writer"}, {"node": "writer", "draft_length": 3196, "revision_count": 0}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "critic"}, {"node": "critic", "approved": false}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "writer"}, {"node": "writer", "draft_length": 3727, "revision_count": 1}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "critic"}, {"node": "critic", "approved": false}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "writer"}, {"node": "writer", "draft_length": 3572, "revision_count": 2}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "critic"}, {"node": "critic", "approved": true}, {"node": "supervisor", "routed_to": "done"}]}
